      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "No need for \"I\" or \"you\": the ending says it, just like in Spanish. Ego (I) and tū (you) are added only for emphasis."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "roots",
        "text": "Spanish amar and ver come from amāre and vidēre, and Spanish haber from habēre. Italian keeps them closer still: amare, vedere, avere. French: aimer, voir, avoir."
      },
      {
        "type": "twist",
        "title": "Questions with -ne",
        "a": [
          "Marcus Rōmam amat.",
          "Marcus loves Rome."
        ],
        "b": [
          "Amatne Marcus Rōmam?",
          "Does Marcus love Rome?"
        ],
        "note": "Latin makes a yes or no question by adding -ne to the first word, usually the verb. There's no \"do\", just as in Spanish."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "culture",
        "text": "Roman names: Marcus Tullius Cicerō had a first name (Marcus), a family name (Tullius) and a nickname that stuck. Cicerō means \"chickpea\", perhaps after an ancestor's nose."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "title": "Verbs in this chapter",
        "rows": [
          [
            "amāre",
            "to love"
          ],
          [
            "laudāre",
            "to praise"
          ],
